/// Behavioral API documentation for the dPassword vault backend.
/// Static Markdown authored from the current backend source; reads no state.
mixin () {
  public query func getApiDoc() : async Text {
    "# dPassword Vault API\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis canister is the storage backend for dPassword, a zero-knowledge notes and password manager. It persists **only** opaque ciphertext and its initialization vector (IV), keyed by the owning principal. No plaintext note or password ever reaches this canister; all encryption and decryption happen in the user's browser.\n\n## Authentication\n\nEvery vault method requires a **signed (non-anonymous) caller**. The app's frontend authenticates through Internet Identity and pins a derivation origin, published at `/.well-known/ii-derivation-origin`. An agent already holding the user's Internet Identity authorization derives the correct per-app principal against that origin (for example `icp identity link web <name> --app <host>`). Such a delegation acts with the user's full authority in this app until it expires.\n\nRegistration is a prerequisite for role-guarded calls. A direct API caller must call `_initialize_access_control` once as a signed-in caller before any role-guarded call, including guarded queries. The first initializer receives the `#admin` role; every subsequent caller receives `#user`. An anonymous caller receives `#guest` from `getCallerUserRole`. A caller that never signed in through the app's own frontend is unregistered even when it belongs to the app's owner, and a signed-in caller derived against a different origin is a different principal than the one the frontend registered.\n\n## Authorization\n\nVault data is scoped strictly to the owning principal. A caller can only read, update, or delete items they own. Another user's items are never returned or modifiable. The vault methods themselves require only a signed-in caller; ownership is enforced per item by the storage layer.\n\n## Methods\n\n### `store_encrypted_item(request : StoreItemRequest) : async Result<ItemId, VaultError>`\n\nCreates a new item when `request.id` is `null`, or updates the caller's existing item when an id is supplied. Returns the item id. On update, `createdAt` is preserved and `updatedAt` is refreshed. Supplying an id the caller does not own returns `#notFound`.\n\n### `get_encrypted_items() : async [ItemSummary]`\n\nReturns the caller's item summaries (id, kind, timestamps) with no payload bytes, newest first.\n\n### `get_encrypted_item(id : ItemId) : async Result<EncryptedItem, VaultError>`\n\nReturns one owned item including its ciphertext and IV. Returns `#notFound` when the caller does not own an item with that id.\n\n### `delete_item(id : ItemId) : async Result<(), VaultError>`\n\nRemoves an owned item. Returns `#notFound` when the caller does not own an item with that id.\n\n## Units and Encoding\n\n- `ItemId` is a `Nat`.\n- `Timestamp` is nanoseconds since the Unix epoch, as returned by `Time.now()` (signed `Int`).\n- `ciphertext` and `iv` are `Blob` byte arrays produced by the browser's client-side encryption.\n- `ItemKind` is the variant `#secureNote` or `#vaultPassword`.\n- `VaultError` is the variant `#notFound : ItemId` or `#notAuthorized`.\n\n## Lifecycle and Polling\n\nItems are created, updated, and deleted synchronously; there is no asynchronous job to poll. `get_encrypted_items` and `get_encrypted_item` are `query` calls and read the latest committed state.\n\n## Mutation Retry Safety\n\n`store_encrypted_item` with a `null` id is **not** idempotent: each call allocates a new id and creates a new item. Retrying a create after an uncertain failure can produce a duplicate item. To update safely, pass the id returned by the original create. `delete_item` is idempotent in effect but returns `#notFound` on a second call for the same id.\n\n## Errors and Gotchas\n\n- Anonymous callers are rejected by the vault methods.\n- Ownership is enforced per item; ids are globally unique but only the owner can act on an item.\n- The canister never sees plaintext and cannot recover a lost client-side key.\n";
  };
};
